<div class="start-card"><p><strong>You will learn:</strong> the relationship between a Deployment, Pod, Service, probes and a rollout.</p><p><strong>Starting requirements:</strong> stages 03–05 pass; images exist in ACR and the kubelet can pull them.</p><p><strong>Shell and directory:</strong> Bash, repository root. For manual commands use <code>export KUBECONFIG="$PWD/workshop/.state/kubeconfig"</code> in each new terminal.</p></div>
<h2 id="manifests">Read the Kubernetes resources</h2>
<p>{{file:k8s/products.yaml}} declares a namespace, an annotated service account, a bootstrap ConfigMap and a Deployment with <strong>one replica</strong>. The Deployment controller keeps the desired Pod running. A Pod is the smallest Kubernetes scheduling unit; here it contains one Java container. Its labels connect the Deployment selector, the Service and the identity webhook.</p>
<p>The Pod requests 500 millicores and 768 MiB so the scheduler can reserve capacity; its larger limits bound usage. Startup allows time for configuration, PostgreSQL connection and Flyway migration. Readiness includes database health; liveness checks whether the process is alive. A temporary database outage should remove the Pod from ready traffic rather than cause a liveness restart loop.</p>
<p>{{file:k8s/service.yaml}} is a <code>LoadBalancer</code> Service: a stable entry point whose selector finds Products Pods. Port 443 forwards to the container's HTTPS port 8443. It restricts callers to the configured public IPv4 <code>/32</code>. A Service forwards traffic; it does not run application code.</p>
<h2 id="apply">Create the endpoint and deploy</h2>
{{snippet:commands.sh:products}}
<p>The script applies the namespace and Service first, waits boundedly for the public IP, and generates a short-lived self-signed certificate for that IP. It installs the certificate/private key as a Kubernetes TLS Secret, renders only known non-secret manifest values, applies the Deployment, waits for rollout and checks HTTPS readiness using the saved certificate.</p>
<div class="warning notice"><p><strong>Endpoint assumptions:</strong> this is a real public HTTPS endpoint, restricted by source IP and requiring sign-in. Its certificate is self-signed and valid for two days. Compare the script's SHA-256 fingerprint with the browser's certificate before accepting the warning. Do not disable TLS verification globally. The supplied tools trust only <code>workshop/.state/tls.crt</code>. Production needs a trusted certificate, DNS, SSO and an access design.</p></div>
<p>The script prints the actual URL and saves it in <code>.state/endpoint.txt</code>. Open it in your browser. Use username <code>participant</code>. Deliberately retrieve the random password with <code>bash workshop/scripts/credentials.sh</code> in your own interactive terminal; the helper refuses redirected output. Do not paste the password into this documentation site's search, progress storage or a URL.</p>
<h2 id="observe">Inspect a working rollout</h2>
<p>After setting this terminal's kubeconfig as above, run <code>kubectl get deployment,pods,service -n products</code>. Expect one ready Products replica, a Pod whose name was generated by the Deployment, and a load balancer IP. Run <code>kubectl logs deployment/products -n products --tail=50</code> and find the Flyway schema creation/validation and Java startup lines. The exact Pod name and output will differ.</p>
<p>Use <code>kubectl describe pod -n products -l app=products</code> to inspect events, identity-injected variables, mounted volumes and probe configuration. Pod events explain image-pull, scheduling and readiness failures more directly than repeatedly refreshing the browser.</p>
<p>Try <code>kubectl rollout restart deployment/products -n products</code> followed by <code>kubectl rollout status deployment/products -n products --timeout=300s</code>. It starts a new Pod from the same image and persisted database; Flyway should not create the schema again. The core has one node, so a rolling update depends on the spare capacity left by the small resource requests.</p>
<h2 id="upload-state">Open Upload product, but do not index yet</h2>
<p>The empty product list and upload form should render. It is possible to upload now, but Event Grid is not subscribed until the next chapter. To avoid a stranded demonstration upload, wait until the Function and subscription are ready before creating your first product. Event Grid does not automatically replay a BlobCreated event that happened before the subscription existed.</p>
<div class="verification"><strong>Verification:</strong> open the real URL, sign in, and see both “Upload product” and “Chat about products”. The readiness request must report <code>UP</code>. Restart Products once and confirm it returns ready with its existing schema.</div>
<h3>Common failures and exact next checks</h3><ul><li><strong>ImagePullBackOff:</strong> use <code>kubectl describe pod</code>; compare the rendered ACR image/tag with the push and verify the kubelet principal's AcrPull assignment. The workload's blob identity does not pull the image.</li><li><strong>CrashLoopBackOff:</strong> run <code>kubectl logs -n products deployment/products --previous</code> if a container restarted. Check App Configuration, Key Vault and private PostgreSQL reachability in that order.</li><li><strong>Browser cannot reach the IP:</strong> confirm the current network still has the configured public IPv4. A changed VPN/egress IP will be blocked. Update <code>allowedCidr</code> to the new exact /32 and rerun the stage; never replace it with the entire internet.</li><li><strong>Certificate expires:</strong> rerun stage 06; it regenerates a near-expiry certificate. Restart Products to load a changed mounted certificate, then compare its new fingerprint.</li><li><strong>403 on a write API:</strong> CSRF protection remains enabled. The UI uses it automatically; smoke/load clients first request a session token from <code>/api/csrf</code>.</li></ul>
<p><strong>Continue when:</strong> Products is ready and accessible. Next, connect the upload event to actual embedding work.</p>
